# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# VMS — turnkey environment.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && docker compose up -d
#
# works as-is: browse http://<any address of this box>:8091 (the default
# API_PORT) and log in as 'admin' with the password ./vms generated into
# VMS_ADMIN_PASSWORD below. Every value below has a working default — the only one worth
# setting on day one is SERVER_IP. Harden the secrets before real deployment:
# ./scripts/gen-secrets.sh
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Docker Desktop hosts only (macOS / Windows) ──────────────────────────────
# Uncomment so plain `docker compose …` includes the bridge overlay (same as
# ./vms auto-detect). Leave commented on native-Linux servers.
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.bridge.yml

# ── Network ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LAN IP of THIS server. Baked into the relay URLs handed to consumers
# (rtsp://<SERVER_IP>:8654/<slug>) — with the loopback default everything on
# the box works (UI, recording, playback), but external consumers (DeepStream
# etc.) get URLs only usable locally. Set your real IP to serve the LAN.
SERVER_IP=127.0.0.1
RTSP_PORT=8654
# Single source of truth for the app/UI port — drives the API bind, the
# healthcheck, the bridge-mode host mapping, the Caddy TLS front door, and the
# dev-server proxy. Change it here only; nothing else hardcodes it.
API_PORT=8091

# ── Database / cache ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Password appears in BOTH lines — keep them identical. NOTE: postgres keeps
# the password it was first initialised with; changing it here later does NOT
# update an existing database volume.
POSTGRES_USER=rtsp
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=rtsp_secret
POSTGRES_DB=rtsp_relay
POSTGRES_PORT=5433
DATABASE_URL=postgresql+asyncpg://rtsp:rtsp_secret@127.0.0.1:5433/rtsp_relay
REDIS_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6380/0

# ── Which services start ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# docker compose reads COMPOSE_PROFILES from this file, so `./vms up -d` and
# `./vms down` both cover every service without --profile flags. Passing them
# by hand is where two failures come from: forgetting them on `down` leaves
# orphaned containers holding the network, and forgetting `analytics` on `up`
# leaves the index with nothing feeding it while every container reports
# healthy.
#
#   search      searchdb + smartsearch  (the index and its query API)
#   frames      vms_frames              (one decode per camera, shared by the
#                                        motion service and analytics)
#   analytics   vms_analytics           (detection, plates, tracking)
#
# THE THREE ARE A SET, not a menu. Smart Search runs with
# SEARCH_FRAME_SOURCE=none — its only mode — so analytics is the only thing
# that detects, and frames is what feeds analytics. Drop all three to run
# without Smart Search entirely (no idle containers, no model images); existing
# installs upgrading keep their old behaviour until they add it, deliberately —
# it changes what personal data the appliance collects.
COMPOSE_PROFILES=search,frames,analytics
# A SEPARATE database from the registry above — see the searchdb comment in
# docker-compose.yml for why it is not pgvector bolted onto POSTGRES_*.
SEARCHDB_USER=search
SEARCHDB_PASSWORD=search_secret
SEARCHDB_NAME=smartsearch
SEARCHDB_PORT=5434
SEARCHDB_URL=postgresql://search:search_secret@127.0.0.1:5434/smartsearch
# How long a crop stays searchable. Applied at insert as expires_at, so it can
# become per-camera later without rewriting rows.
SEARCH_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# How often the service deletes rows past expires_at and unlinks their crops.
# This is what makes SEARCH_RETENTION_DAYS a real limit rather than a stamp:
# without a sweep the rows and JPEGs accumulate forever. 0 disables the thread
# for a deployment that runs services/smartsearch/scripts/retention.py from
# cron instead — searches filter out expired rows either way, so the cost of
# disabling is disk, never wrong answers.
SEARCH_RETENTION_SWEEP_SECONDS=3600
# Sweep crop files that no row references every Nth pass (24 = daily at the
# default interval). They accumulate when a write_batch insert fails after the
# JPEG was written — nothing in the database points at them, so only this finds
# them. Files younger than an hour are never candidates: crops are written
# before their rows, so a brand-new file with no row is normal. 0 disables.
SEARCH_RETENTION_ORPHAN_EVERY_N=24
SMARTSEARCH_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1
SMARTSEARCH_PORT=8013
SEARCH_CROP_DIR=/data/search/crops
# Seconds with NO camera registered for indexing before the detector, encoder
# and plate reader are released. Smart Search costs ~2.9 GB and a busy core
# while it is indexing and nearly the same while it is not, so the weights are
# loaded when the first camera is enabled and given back when the last is
# disabled. Searching already-indexed footage keeps working throughout — the
# encoder reloads in ~1.7 s to serve a query.
#
# This is a DEBOUNCE: a camera URL change is a remove-then-add, so the count
# touches zero during ordinary registry traffic. Do not drop it below ~60.
# Set 0 to load once and hold forever, which is what a busy site wants.
SEARCH_IDLE_TIMEOUT=300
# ── sampling, tracking and indexing ──────────────────────────────────────────
# The rate the frame source is expected to run at. Smart Search itself no
# longer samples (SEARCH_FRAME_SOURCE=none, below); /health reports this as
# `expected_source_fps`, so it has to match FRAMES_SAMPLE_FPS or the health
# page shows a mismatch that is not real.
#
# 5 FPS is for the OBJECT TRACKER and for nothing else. Measured by replaying
# the same 16 NVR segments: 1 -> 2 halved association failure while distinct
# objects found rose only 89 -> 91 against detections 969 -> 1989, and 2 -> 5
# cut recorded association failure 14.9% -> 9.2% for about 1.4x broker CPU.
# Sampling faster buys IDENTITY, not coverage.
SEARCH_SAMPLE_FPS=5
# ── DETECTION LIVES IN THE ANALYTICS SERVICE ─────────────────────────────────
# Smart Search no longer decodes, detects or reads plates. It embeds crops and
# searches the embeddings; services/analytics finds the objects and posts them.
#
# BOTH HALVES ARE NOW THE DEFAULT, in docker-compose.yml itself: Smart Search
# stops looking at frames (SEARCH_FRAME_SOURCE=none, below) AND analytics gets
# a sink URL. Either alone is a fault — one records everything twice, the other
# records nothing — and shipping only the second half is what left a clean
# install with an index that could never grow while every service reported
# healthy. docker-compose.analytics.yml still exists and still applies the same
# pair; it is no longer required to make a default install work.
#
# `none` is the only valid value now. An existing .env carrying `sampler` or
# `broker` fails at startup with an explanation rather than indexing silently
# into nothing.
SEARCH_FRAME_SOURCE=none

# Where analytics gets frames: broker | sampler. Broker is the default and
# means it reads the frame the vms_frames service already decoded, with the
# canonical motion analysis already run on those exact pixels.
ANALYTICS_FRAME_SOURCE=broker

# ONE OBJECT MUST NOT BECOME TWO. Same-domain boxes nested inside one another —
# a torso inside a full body — are collapsed to the outer box before the
# tracker or appearance dedup can call them two objects. This is the
# containment at which that happens; 0 turns it off so you can compare on a
# live site without a rebuild. Keep it in step with
# services/analytics/config.yaml.
#ANALYTICS_NESTED_CONTAINMENT=0.9

# Where analytics sends accepted observations. DEFAULTED ON in
# docker-compose.yml (127.0.0.1:8013 on host networking, smartsearch:8013 on
# the bridge overlay) — leave this commented out and it just works.
#
# Set it to an EXPLICIT EMPTY VALUE to run analytics beside an older
# deployment: it then detects, tracks and decides, reports its counts on
# /health, and sends nothing. Only do that if something else is indexing, or
# nothing will be searchable.
#ANALYTICS_SINK_URL=

# How long analytics holds its weights with NO camera asking for detection.
#
# The detector and the plate reader are ingest-only: they load on the first
# camera and are released when the last one goes, so switching Person, Vehicles
# and ANPR off across the fleet is a real saving and not just a pause. The
# plate reader has its own demand signal on top of this — it is loaded only
# while some camera selects the `plate` domain, because it is a second model
# over every vehicle.
#
# THE TIMER IS A DEBOUNCE, NOT A TUNING KNOB. camera-mgmt reconciles from every
# uvicorn worker and a domain change is a remove followed by an add, so the
# registered count touches zero on ordinary traffic. Below ~60 s this starts
# unloading and reloading for no reason. Set 0 to load once and hold for ever,
# which is what a permanently busy site wants.
#ANALYTICS_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=300

# Where the Motion service gets its signal: capture | broker.
#
#   capture  open our own RTSP session per camera and difference the frames.
#            What has always shipped, and the default.
#   broker   take the changed-pixel fraction from the vms_frames notice. This
#            service never needed the pixels — only one float per sample — so
#            a full 1080p decode per camera was pure duplication.
#
# OPERATOR-VISIBLE BEHAVIOUR IS UNCHANGED. Both paths call the same
# worker.apply_feature: same 3 s confirmation window, same TRIGGERED/
# MONITORING machine, same cooldown, same Events API. The scalar the broker
# publishes is verified bit-identical to what this service would have
# computed, on 16:9 and non-16:9 cameras alike
# (services/frames/scripts/scalar_parity.py).
#
# Only set this to broker with `--profile frames` running. With no notices
# arriving, cameras report CONNECTING and raise no events — silence, not
# invented MONITORING.
MOTION_FRAME_SOURCE=capture

# Size of the tmpfs volume the broker writes frames into (broker mode only).
# 5 cameras x 4 slots x 6.22 MB = 124 MB at 1080p, so raise this with CAMERA
# COUNT, not with slot count. /health reports free space and warns before a
# ring allocation would fail.
FRAMES_SHM_SIZE=256m
# Object identity. false falls back to one-track-per-detection, which disables
# indexing suppression in practice since nothing can be recognised as a repeat.
SEARCH_TRACKING_ENABLED=true
# Maximum association cost for a detection to continue a track. Real
# continuations measured at p99 = 1.63, so this covers ~99% of them. Raising it
# reduces track fragmentation but starts MERGING distinct objects, which is the
# worse failure for search — see services/smartsearch/config.yaml for the sweep.
SEARCH_TRACK_MAX_COST=1.75
# Seconds a track survives with no matching detection.
SEARCH_TRACK_MAX_AGE=3
# THE SUPPRESSION ESCAPE HATCH. false indexes every observation of every track,
# which is what to set when you suspect suppression is hiding something.
SEARCH_INDEX_SUPPRESSION_ENABLED=true
# Re-index once an object has moved this far FROM WHERE IT WAS LAST INDEXED
# (object heights), not from where it was last seen — the main gain of the
# tracker over the temporal gate it replaced.
SEARCH_INDEX_DISPLACEMENT=1.5
# Re-index a visible but motionless object at least this often. 0 = OFF, which
# is the default on evidence: measured over 14 minutes of continuous footage it
# fired once in 322 observations and moved coverage by 0.1 points, because
# tracks rarely live that long (median 2.5 s) and because CLIP dedup rejects
# most heartbeat crops as identical. Keeping a still object findable is already
# done by the first-sight rule. Set a positive number to re-enable — the
# mechanism is intact and still tested.
SEARCH_INDEX_HEARTBEAT=0
# Motion-gate sensitivity preset: low | medium | high. Unset keeps the measured
# explicit values. "high" is NOT better — measured, it found 6 of 8 people where
# medium found all 8, because more crops make the deduplicator merge people.
# SEARCH_MOTION_SENSITIVITY=medium
# Detector implementation: auto | openvino | torch.
#
# AUTO MEANS OPENVINO ON CPU, measured rather than assumed. Live 1920x1080
# camera frames, 15 timed rounds after a discarded warm-up:
#
#     torch      p50 40.8ms   p95  62.0ms
#     openvino   p50 17.6ms   p95  21.8ms   <- 2.3x faster, and steadier
#     onnx       p50 89.4ms   p95 215.9ms   <- 2.2x SLOWER; not offered
#
# AUTO picks torch on CUDA (the OpenVINO build here is CPU-only). A failed
# OpenVINO load falls through to torch inside the same warm-up, so it never
# costs indexing; SEARCH_BACKEND_ATTEMPTS caps how many warm-ups keep trying.
#
# Worth keeping in proportion: at 16 cameras the detector was ~4% of this
# container's CPU. The rest is RTSP decode and the motion gate, which no
# backend choice touches. See services/smartsearch/scripts/capacity.py.
SEARCH_DETECTOR_BACKEND=auto
SEARCH_BACKEND_ATTEMPTS=3
# Square-letterbox the detector input instead of ultralytics' rectangular
# padding. Exported backends (ONNX, OpenVINO) have a FIXED square input and
# pad that way whatever this says, so matching them is what lets a backend be
# swapped without changing what is detected.
#
# IT ALSO CHANGES THE CURRENT TORCH PATH. Measured 2026-09-04: more marginal
# detections at the frame edge — 4 objects became 5 on a test photograph — so
# expect more crops, embeddings and stored rows. Set false to restore the
# previous behaviour; it takes a container restart, not a rebuild.
SEARCH_SQUARE_LETTERBOX=true
# ANPR: the "validation deferred" audit that stood here is SUPERSEDED. It said
# plate reading had never run because no localiser weights existed anywhere.
# That is no longer the situation — an open baseline detector now ships and
# auto-downloads, so plate reading works on a first start. What has still not
# happened is an end-to-end read validated against real plates on this
# deployment; treat plates.available=true as "a detector is loadable", not as
# "reads have been checked".

# Plate reading (ANPR). Works out of the box: an open plate detector
# (open-image-models, MIT) auto-downloads on first use, like every other model.
# UNSET SEARCH_PLATE_MODEL = that open baseline; EXPLICITLY EMPTY
# (SEARCH_PLATE_MODEL=) = plate reading off entirely.
# SEARCH_PLATE_WEIGHTS points at site-tuned detection weights (a .pt inside
# the search_models volume, e.g. /models/my-anpr.pt) and beats the baseline
# whenever set — this is where a region-trained model plugs in.
#SEARCH_PLATE_MODEL=
#SEARCH_PLATE_WEIGHTS=
# The WRITE side: which index this VMS feeds cameras to. Distinct from
# SMARTSEARCH_API_URL below, which is the index it QUERIES.
# UNSET (the default here) = the co-located smartsearch service (the compose
# files pick the right address per network mode: 127.0.0.1:8013 on Linux host
# networking, smartsearch:8013 on the Docker Desktop bridge).
# SET EXPLICITLY EMPTY (SMARTSEARCH_INDEX_URL=) = this VMS feeds no index.
#SMARTSEARCH_INDEX_URL=

# The same for the plate localiser, and OFF by default on purpose. The two-line
# ANPR thresholds were fitted to 109 real reads under RECTANGULAR preprocessing,
# and square padding changes which plate regions are boxed and how much margin
# they carry — margin being exactly what the recogniser needs. This switch is
# for RUNNING that validation on a real site, not for turning the change on
# before it has been done.
SEARCH_PLATE_SQUARE_LETTERBOX=false

MEDIAMTX_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:9987
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ── Identity / OIDC (Keycloak — the single login for the whole product) ───────
# Turnkey mode: the SPA reaches Keycloak on the same hostname the browser used,
# and the API accepts tokens issued via localhost, 127.0.0.1, or SERVER_IP —
# so login works on the box AND from the LAN with no changes here.
# DEV_AUTH=true bypasses login entirely (dev only).
DEV_AUTH=false
KEYCLOAK_PORT=8085
# Realm login: user 'admin'. The realm file seeds it with the literal password
# 'admin', which sync-client.py retires on the first `up` in favour of
# VMS_ADMIN_PASSWORD below — that is the password to type.
# Keycloak master-console admin:
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN=admin
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin

# ── The application logins (NOT the Keycloak console above) ──────────────────
# deploy/keycloak/realm-vms.json seeds the users a fresh appliance can be signed
# into with: `admin` (admin+operator+dpo) always, and `operator` only where the
# realm file still carries it (the public tree's realm seeds admin alone). Each
# ships with its username as a literal password.
#
# scripts/gen-secrets.sh generates a password for each seeded user on first run
# (VMS_OPERATOR_PASSWORD stays empty when there is no operator), and
# deploy/keycloak/sync-client.py applies them on the first `up`, retiring the
# shipped literals. Each user is then marked so a later `up` never overwrites a
# password chosen since. Further accounts: see HARDENING.md §1.
#
# LEAVE THESE EMPTY AND THE SHIPPED PASSWORDS STAY LIVE. The sync logs a
# warning saying so. On an appliance that already imported its realm, changing
# them here does nothing — use the console or the self-service page.
VMS_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
VMS_OPERATOR_PASSWORD=
#
# Optional pins — leave unset for turnkey. Set BOTH only when fronting with a
# fixed URL (e.g. TLS via Caddy: docker compose --profile tls up -d caddy):
#   OIDC_ISSUER=https://<SERVER_IP>:8444/realms/vms
#   OIDC_PUBLIC_URL=https://<SERVER_IP>:8444
#
# Per-front-door Keycloak URLs — needed when the product is reached through a
# reverse proxy / tunnel that forwards only 443, NOT Keycloak's :8085 (e.g. a
# Cloudflare tunnel). Maps the app hostname the browser used → the public
# Keycloak base URL, so the SPA talks to a reachable endpoint instead of
# guessing <host>:8085. Hosts NOT listed fall through to the turnkey
# <host>:8085 derivation, so LAN access is untouched and both work at once. The
# API also accepts tokens issued by every mapped URL. Requires a DNS record for
# the auth host and a tunnel/proxy route sending it to :8085.
# Format: app_host=auth_url,app_host2=auth_url2
#   OIDC_PUBLIC_HOSTS=vms.example.com=https://auth-vms.example.com
# Logout via a tunnel/proxy needs no extra config: the vms-web client's
# post-logout redirect URIs are reconciled to "*" on every `up` by
# deploy/keycloak/sync-client.py, so any front door that can log in can log out.
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=vms-web
OIDC_AUDIENCE=
CORS_ORIGINS=

# ── Camera discovery (runs inside the api service; /api/discovery/*) ─────────
# No network configuration needed: an auto scan sweeps the subnets derived
# from the box's own interfaces plus the networks of every camera already in
# the registry. (For the rare routed camera VLAN neither source can see, the
# DISCOVERY_SUBNETS env var accepts extra comma-separated CIDRs.)
# Derives the Fernet key that encrypts stored camera credentials at rest.
# Keep it STABLE once cameras are onboarded — rotating it means re-entering
# camera credentials.
DISCOVERY_SECRET_KEY=dev-discovery-secret-change-for-production
# ONVIF WSDL for the Media2 (H.265) stream-settings path — baked into the image.
# ONVIF_MEDIA2_WSDL=/app/backend/onvif_wsdl/media2.wsdl

# ── NVR (recording) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NVR_PORT=8009
NVR_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8009
# 127.0.0.1 = NVR reachable only via the authenticated /api/nvr proxy (default).
# 0.0.0.0   = also reachable raw on the LAN (needed only for legacy consumers
#             like an external clip-service; the NVR API itself has NO auth).
NVR_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1
# Retention applied to cameras auto-synced from the registry
NVR_DEFAULT_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# HARD cap on total recorded footage (GB). When exceeded, the oldest segments
# across ALL cameras are deleted (hourly pass) until back under. Size it to
# ~85-90% of the disk backing the nvr_data volume (in-flight segments and
# clip exports need headroom). Empty = uncapped — age-based retention only.
#
# DOCKER DESKTOP ON WINDOWS: size it against the HOST drive, not against what
# the Storage screen reports. The nvr_data volume lives in a dynamically
# expanding vhdx with a ~1 TB virtual maximum, so the NVR sees ~1 TB free while
# the real limit is whatever is free on the drive holding the vhdx (by default
# C:, under %LOCALAPPDATA%\Docker). Taking 85-90% of the reported size there
# yields a cap roughly ten times larger than the host can honour. Worse, every
# automatic protection is a percentage of that same inflated total — the 90%/95%
# disk meters and the disk-floor evictor all stay quiet while C: fills to
# completion, so this cap is the ONLY thing standing between one camera at
# ~10 GB/day and a full system drive. The NVR logs a warning at boot when it
# detects this. Either set a value the host drive can genuinely supply, or move
# Docker Desktop's disk image to a larger drive (Settings → Resources →
# Advanced → Disk image location).
#
# The Storage admin screen offers the largest value the volume can actually
# hold and refuses anything above it; this seed is applied verbatim instead,
# and an oversized value is only logged at boot. A cap larger than the disk is
# not a larger limit — the NVR evicts footage at its disk floor regardless of
# the cap, so the number is recorded and never enforced. Prefer the UI, or
# leave this empty and set it there.
NVR_MAX_STORAGE_GB=
# ── Storage pressure alerting ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The NVR evaluates disk AND cap pressure on a timer and POSTs a JSON body here
# when the state CHANGES (ok → warning → critical and back), never on every
# check — a standing alert repeated every minute is one an operator filters
# away. Empty = log only, which is still a supported deployment.
#
# It exists because every other storage warning needs someone looking at a
# screen: the header meter, the Storage tab, and the WARNING/CRITICAL log lines.
# An appliance nobody logs into for a month has no warning at all without this.
#
# Stdlib urllib, so it works identically on Linux, macOS and Windows and adds no
# dependency to the NVR.
NVR_ALERT_WEBHOOK_URL=
NVR_ALERT_INTERVAL_SECONDS=60
# Registry→NVR sync (recording starts automatically for enabled cameras)
NVR_SYNC_ENABLED=true
NVR_SYNC_INTERVAL=60
# ── Motion detection service ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CPU-only motion analysis of relay streams for cameras with the per-camera
# Motion detection option on (Edit camera → Motion detection). Reached only
# via the authenticated /api/motion proxy.
MOTION_PORT=8012
MOTION_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8012
MOTION_BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1
MOTION_SYNC_ENABLED=true

# ── Smart Search (CLIP index) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The natural-language index over analytics crops, reached only via the
# authenticated /api/search proxy (which also scopes results to cameras this
# VMS records, enforces the smart_search permission, and audits every query).
# It has no auth of its own, so narrow its network exposure to the appliances
# that proxy for it.
#
# UNSET (the default here) = the co-located smartsearch service the stack now
# starts by default, so a first install is searchable out of the box.
# SET EXPLICITLY EMPTY (SMARTSEARCH_API_URL=) = this VMS runs no index; the
# Smart Search page reports itself as not configured rather than broken, and
# recording, playback and analytics are unaffected.
# SET TO A URL = a remote index (e.g. a separate GPU host).
#SMARTSEARCH_API_URL=
# Sent as X-API-Key when set. Empty = no header (the service checks none yet).
SMARTSEARCH_API_KEY=

# ── Branding (white-label) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The product name, in ONE place. It reaches three surfaces from here:
#   • the console SPA  — sidebar, login loader, legal note, page-title fallback
#   • the browser tab  — <title>, substituted at build time
#   • the sign-in page — via the realm displayName, reconciled on every `up`
#
# The SPA is compiled inside the api image, so a change here needs a REBUILD to
# show up in the console:  ./vms build api && ./vms up -d
# The sign-in page needs no rebuild — keycloak_client_sync applies it at start.
# Leave blank to keep the shipped defaults. QUOTE any value containing a
# space — compose strips the quotes, and it keeps `set -a; . .env` working.
BRAND_NAME=
BRAND_SITE=

# AGPL-3.0 §13 source link shown in the console footer. Until this points at a
# real repository the footer omits the link entirely (see src/lib/legal.ts).
SOURCE_URL=

# ── DeepStream analytics pipeline (same appliance) ───────────────────────────
# THE ONE LINE TO CHANGE IF THE PIPELINE CHECKOUT MOVES. Root of that checkout
# on this host — the directory holding its docker-compose.yml. Everything
# host-side is derived from it: docker-compose.yml bind-mounts
# <DEEPSTREAM_HOME>/config/camera_config into the api container, which writes
# one <slug>.json per camera there and the pipeline scans on boot. The two
# processes exchange config through the filesystem rather than the network (no
# auth, no startup ordering, and the config survives either service being
# down). The API is the ONLY writer of that directory; the pipeline reads it.
#
# Matches docker-compose.yml's own fallback, so leaving it alone is coherent
# rather than merely unset. It previously shipped one developer's home
# directory as the documented default, which every install then carried.
DEEPSTREAM_HOME=/opt/deepstream
# Escape hatch, normally left commented out: set only if the config directory
# does not sit at <DEEPSTREAM_HOME>/config/camera_config. Overrides the derived
# path outright, so DEEPSTREAM_HOME is then ignored for the mount.
#DEEPSTREAM_CONFIG_DIR_HOST=
DEEPSTREAM_PROJECTION_ENABLED=true
# The same files as the PIPELINE container sees them — its add/modify endpoints
# take a path and open it themselves, so they need their own view of the mount.
# NOT derived from DEEPSTREAM_HOME and unaffected by moving the checkout: the
# pipeline's own compose mounts `.` at a fixed container path, so this changes
# only if that compose file's volumes/working_dir change.
DEEPSTREAM_PIPELINE_CONFIG_DIR=/apps/deepstream-yolo-e2e/config/camera_config
# The pipeline's REST API — written configs are pushed here so an operator's
# zone edit goes live at once rather than at the pipeline's next restart.
DEEPSTREAM_API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8081
# Drift repair only — edits apply immediately via the request-path hooks. Also
# re-adds cameras the pipeline lost to a restart (its state is in-memory).
DEEPSTREAM_PROJECTION_INTERVAL=300

# Container-local timezone: the NVR grooming window (groom_window) runs in this
# timezone, and segment filenames are stamped with it. Set to the site's zone.
TZ=Asia/Kolkata

# ── Internal machine-to-machine key (trusted callers of the api) ─────────────
# Generate for production: openssl rand -base64 48 | tr -d '/+='
INTERNAL_API_KEY=dev-internal-key-change-for-production
